<h2>The counter-argument: "I'm strong enough from CrossFit, I just need to survive the run"</h2>

<p>The most common pushback to dedicated run periodization in HYROX is the belief that raw strength solves everything. The argument is that if you can back squat 180 kilograms and do unbroken wall balls, the stations will demand such a low percentage of your maximum capacity that you won't be tired for the run.</p>

<p>There is a grain of truth here: a massive strength reserve makes the sleds mathematically easier. If you are weak, <a href="/en/blog/how-to-program-hyrox-run-transitions-sled-fatigue/">the sleds will stop you entirely</a>.</p>

<p>But the counter-argument fails on the track. A heavy back squat does not build mitochondrial density. It does not increase the rate at which your body clears lactate from the bloodstream. When you step off the sleds, your heart rate will be at 175 beats per minute regardless of how strong you are. If you have not built the specific aerobic engine to clear that fatigue while running at 4:30/km, your strength won't save you. You will simply be a very strong athlete walking the final three kilometers.</p>

<p>HYROX is an endurance race. Treat the run with the respect it demands, structure your zones, master the float intervals, and let the rest of your age group blow up on the sleds.</p>
